panvk: VK_EXT_external_memory_host on Darwin, for the pages of IOSurfaces and of the process's own

Applied by tools/mk-panvk.sh only, after tools/mesa/*.patch and the panvk patches before it, to Mesa 25.0.7 (Mesa's own
licence, MIT, applies to the lines it adds). Written for Scion, after tools/mesa/turnip-darwin.patch's import for Turnip.

Why. Metal's no-copy buffers (newBufferWithBytesNoCopy:), and every texture over an IOSurface, which the frontend
(userland/src/metalvk) makes a no-copy buffer over the surface's pages, are imports of host memory. PanVK imported none, so
metal2vulkan made each one a buffer of its own memory and copied the caller's bytes in and out around every command buffer
(tools/metal/m2v/0002, 0006). The window server on Metal ran at 115 to 166% CPU, idle or not, against GLES's 20 to 72%
(build 54); on build 55, with the surfaces imported, its 137 other no-copy buffers still cost 78 ms a commit copied in and
48 ms compared out (docs/metal.md, phases 5 and 6).

What. On Darwin, PanVK offers VK_EXT_external_memory_host, minImportedHostPointerAlignment 16 KiB (a page of Darwin's on
arm64):
  - An IOSurface's pages, which the frontend told src/hakios-drm of (hakios_drm_host_surface), are imported as the surface's
    buffer, which HakiosMali makes by its ID (kHakiosMaliSurface, what pan_kmod_bo_import already does with a surface ID on
    Darwin), in the one coherent memory type: HakiosMali cleans and invalidates a surface's lines when a submission names
    it and when the display or a WAIT_BO waits for it. A surface whose pixels do not start at its first page, or that is
    smaller than the import, is refused.
  - Other whole pages of the process's own are wired by HakiosMali as a buffer (its request 0x7b, hakios_drm_user_memory,
    pan_kmod_bo_import_user), in a second memory type, HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_CACHED and not coherent (pages the process may
    only read, for the GPU to read only: a job that writes one faults): the Mali does not see the processors' caches, so
    vkFlushMappedMemoryRanges and vkInvalidateMappedMemoryRanges have the kernel clean and invalidate the lines of such
    memory (HakiosMali's request 0x7a, hakios_drm_sync), which metal2vulkan calls around a command buffer's use of it
    (0001, 0008). Memory allocated in that type without an import is the first type's.
  - Only where the kernel has request 0x7a (asked once, of no buffer: ENOENT, not ENOSYS). The process cannot keep the
    lines itself at any useful speed: iOS's sys_dcache_flush() is a DSB alone, and its own DC CIVAC traps with the Exynos
    M3s up (xnu patch 1511), at about 0.67 us a line, which is slower than metal2vulkan's copy. On a kernel without the
    request, those pages are refused, and copied as before. tools/metal/vk_hostcoh.c checks it line by line: with every
    line cleaned and invalidated around each job, nothing is lost; with sys_dcache_flush(), most lines are.
  - vkGetMemoryHostPointerPropertiesEXT: the coherent type for a surface's pages, the cached one for other whole pages
    (where they are imported at all).
  - Buffers and images take either type; external buffer properties and linear 2D images are importable from host
    allocations, never exported.
  - vkMapMemory of imported memory is the caller's pointer, and is never unmapped here.
Anything refused (VK_ERROR_INVALID_EXTERNAL_HANDLE) metal2vulkan copies, as before.

